"Microsoft Word - Abrahamson_Wilensky_JPS20.doc (https://ccl.northwestern.edu/papers/Abrahamson_Wilensky_JPS20.pdf)\nciteturn20view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 49; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ccl.northwestern.edu/papers/Abrahamson_Wilensky_JPS20.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1157\nL0@P0: Piaget? Vygotsky? I’m Game! 1\nL1@P0: Running head: AGENT-BASED MODELING FOR PSYCHOLOGY\nL2@P0: Piaget? Vygotsky? I’m Game!:\nL3@P0: Agent-Based Modeling for Psychology Research\nL4@P0: Dor Abrahamson and Uri Wilensky\nL5@P0: The Center for Connected Learning and Computer-Based Modeling\nL6@P0: Northwestern University\nL7@P0: Contact Information:\nL8@P0: Dor Abrahamson\nL9@P0: Graduate School of Education\nL10@P0: 4649 Tolman Hall\nL11@P0: University of California, Berkeley\nL12@P0: Berkeley, CA 94720-1670\nL13@P0: USA\nL14@P0: TEL: +1 510 643 3599\nL15@P0: FAX: +1 510 642 3769\nL16@P0: e-mail: dor@berkeley.edu\nL17@P0: Paper presented at the annual meeting of the Jean Piaget Society, Vancouver, June 1 – 3, 2005.\nL18@P0: We are currently in the process of revising this conference paper toward submission to a journal. Please\nL19@P0-1: contact the authors with any questions with regards to the current draft.2\nL20@P1: Abstract\nL21@P1: We discuss agent-based models as research tools for developmental and social psychology. “Agents” are\nL22@P1: computer-based entities, e.g., individual people or animals. To construct an agent-based model, the\nL23@P1: modeler assigns the agents real-world roles and rules, and then studies the model through conducting\nL24@P1: simulation experiments in which the agents follow their rules, and observes real-time data. Agent-based\nL25@P1: models are dynamic, expressive, and afford immediate feedback. Agent-based models are particularly\nL26@P1: useful for understanding complex phenomena, e.g., the dynamics of multiple individual learners\nL27@P1: interacting with their peers and with artifacts in their environment and the emergent group patterns\nL28@P1: arising over time from these multiple interactions. We describe an agent-based simulation that we\nL29@P1: designed as a “thought experiment” to shed light on the ongoing debate between two theories of\nL30@P1: learning, constructivism and social constructivism. When “running” the models, unexpected\nL31@P1: consequences arise, and this leads to successive refinement of theory. Practicing ABM in psychology\nL32@P1: research can potentially support the development of richer theoretical models that coordinate\nL33@P1-2: complementary perspectives as viable complements of an integrated explanatory structure.3\nL34@P2: Piaget? Vygotsky? I’m Game!:\nL35@P2: Agent-Based Modeling for Psychology Research\nL36@P2: “[T]he social fact is for us a fact to be explained, not to be\nL37@P2: invoked as an extra-psychological factor.”\nL38@P2: Piaget (1962/1951, p. 4)\nL39@P2: “The development of thought is to Piaget, a story of the\nL40@P2: gradual socialization of deeply intimate, personal, autistic\nL41@P2: mental states. Even social speech is represented as\nL42@P2: following, not preceding, egocentric speech.”\nL43@P2: Vygotsky (1962/1934, p. 18)\nL44@P2: In this paper, we present a new methodology for conducting research on psychology theory,\nL45@P2: agent-based modeling. Agent-based modeling (hence ABM) has been increasingly used by natural\nL46@P2: scientists to study a wide range of phenomena such as the interactions of species in an ecosystem, the\nL47@P2: interactions of molecules in a chemical reaction, the percolation of oil through a substrate, and the food\nL48@P2: gathering behavior of social insects (Sole & Goodwin, 2000; Bonabeau, Dorigo, & Théraulaz, 1999;\nL49@P2: Troisi, Wong & Ratner, 2005; Wilensky & Reisman, 1998, 2006).\nL50@P2: What all these studies have in common that make them particularly conducive to ABM treatment\nL51@P2: is that the focal phenomenon in question can be construed as a pattern resulting from the cumulative\nL52@P2: activity of many elements. For example, the phenomenon of pressure, e.g., in a gas chamber, is\nL53@P2: explained as the cumulative collisions of myriad particles with the walls of the chamber. Such\nL54@P2: phenomena, which lend themselves to two or more layers of description, e.g., particle collisions are the\nL55@P2: “micro” events, and pressure is the “macro” event, have been termed complex, and are collectively\nL56@P2-3: studied in a relatively young interdisciplinary field called complex systems or complexity studies4\nL57@P3: (Axelrod, 1999; Bar-Yam, 1997; Holland, 1995; Kauffman, 1995; Wimsatt, 1986, cited in Clark, 1997,\nL58@P3: p. 114). Typical of complex phenomena is that the cumulative (‘aggregate’) patterns or behaviors at the\nL59@P3: macro level are not premeditated or directly actuated by any of the “lower-level” micro elements. For\nL60@P3: example, flocking birds do not intend to construct an arrow-shaped structure. Rather, each element\nL61@P3: (“agent”) follows its “local” rules, and the overall pattern arises as epiphenomenal to these multiple local\nL62@P3: behaviors—the overall pattern emerges. The agents can be instantiated in the form of a computer\nL63@P3: program that specifies their rule-based behaviors. ABM is thus particularly powerful for studying\nL64@P3: complex phenomena, because once the modeler assigns agents their local rules, the modeler can set\nL65@P3: these virtual agents into motion and watch for any overall patterns that arise from the agents’\nL66@P3: interactions. For example, the modeler might assign a group of virtual birds a set of rules and then watch\nL67@P3: their interactions to see whether typical flock structures emerge (Reynolds, 1987; see also Wilensky,\nL68@P3: 1998).\nL69@P3: Whereas initially complex-systems methods and perspectives arose from the natural sciences,\nL70@P3: complexity, emergence, and micro and macro levels of description of phenomena are all highly relevant\nL71@P3: to research in the social sciences. Indeed, the recent decades have seen a surge in social-science studies\nL72@P3: employing ABM (Epstein & Axtell, 1996; Diermeier, 2000; Axelrod, 1997). In this paper we focus on\nL73@P3: the potential of ABM as research tools for formulating and critiquing social-science theory. We do so by\nL74@P3: looking at a specific example, the debate between Piagetian and Vygotskiian accounts of learning, and\nL75@P3: we introduce our main result as an instance of the methodology we are advocating. We argue that ABM\nL76@P3: has potential to contribute to the advancement of theory in at least three major ways that we illustrate in\nL77@P3: this paper: (a) explicitizing—ABM computational environments demand an exacting level of clarity and\nL78@P3: specificity in expressing a theoretical model and provide the tools, structures, and standard practices to\nL79@P3: achieve this high level; (b) emergence—the computational power of ABM enables the researcher to\nL80@P3-4: mobilize an otherwise static list of conjectured behaviors and witness any group-level patterns that may 5\nL81@P4: enfold through multiple interactions between the agents who implement these conjectured behaviors;\nL82@P4: and (c) intra/inter-disciplinary collaboration—the lingua franca of ABM enables researchers who\nL83@P4: otherwise use different frameworks, terminology, and methodologies to understand and critique each\nL84@P4: others’ theory and even challenge or improve the theory by modifying and/or extending the\nL85@P4: computational procedures that underlie the model. In fact, we invite other researchers to explore the\nL86@P4: advantages of including ABM models with their theories. To demonstrate the plausibility of such model\u0002based discourse, we welcome theory-of-learning researchers to challenge the model that we present in\nL87@P4: this paper.\nL88@P4: 1\nL89@P4: What Is an Agent-Based Model?\nL90@P4: The agent-based model is the researcher’s idealized approximation of ‘how things work in the\nL91@P4: world.’ “Agents” are computer-based entities, e.g., “people.” The modeler creates the agents that are\nL92@P4: conjectured to be instrumental in the real-world phenomenon under study and assigns these agents rules\nL93@P4: that express the modeler’s conjecture as to real-world behaviors. The modeler also creates a virtual\nL94@P4: environment where these agents operate. Next the modeler conducts simulation experiments in which\nL95@P4: the agents play out their rules within the environment and in response to each other. Running these\nL96@P4: simulations is not just an animated enhancement of the programmed rules within a visual medium. The\nL97@P4: dynamic interactivity of these agents is such that it is often very difficult or even impossible to predict\nL98@P4: emergent outcomes from the agents’ individual rules and the environmental attributes. Moreover, the\nL99@P4: random values inherent in each simulation make for different outcomes each time a specific model is\nL100@P4: run. Thus, the craft of ABM involves “informed fiddling”: the modeler iteratively explores within a vast\nL101@P4: range of parameters, including the environmental settings and the logic of the agents’ behaviors, until a\nL102@P4: simulation is run that yields patterns that appear familiar from observations of real-world data. This\nL103@P4: virtual-to-real similarity does not constitute adequate proof of the hypothesized rules, yet it renders the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMicrosoft Word - AERA07-AbrWilLev.doc (https://ccl.northwestern.edu/2007/abr-wil-levin-aera2007.pdf)\nciteturn20view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 9 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 15; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ccl.northwestern.edu/2007/abr-wil-levin-aera2007.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 567\nL0@P0: Abrahamson, Wilensky, & Levin (2007)\nL1@P0: 1\nL2@P0: Agent-Based Modeling as a Bridge Between Cognitive and Social Perspectives on Learning\nL3@P0: Dor Abrahamson, University of California - Berkeley\nL4@P0: Uri Wilensky, Northwestern University\nL5@P0: James Levin, University of California - San Diego\nL6@P0: This paper is a proof-of-existence empirical paper. That said, it is also a methodological paper.\nL7@P0: The methodology is agent-based modeling, a computer-supported mode of inquiry into complex\nL8@P0: phenomena, such as weather fronts, market fluctuations, or participation patterns in a middle\u0002school mathematics lesson. We have previously shown that agent-based simulation can express\nL9@P0: theoretical models of learning (Abrahamson & Wilensky, 2005; see also Smith & Conrey, 2007).\nL10@P0: In that paper, we claimed that a promising attribute of simulation-based research into learning is\nL11@P0: that it fosters scholarly critique and engaging collaboration. It is that claim that is herein proven\nL12@P0: to stand. The proof lies in the collaborative criticism offered by the third author, Jim Levin, to\nL13@P0: the first two authors, Dor Abrahamson and Uri Wilensky, concerning their agent-based\nL14@P0: simulation of learning, which was first presented at the 2005 annual meeting of the Jean Piaget\nL15@P0: Society and was then made available online, along with its underlying computational procedures\nL16@P0: that were laid out for scrutiny. The 2005 paper explicitly invited fellow researchers to critique\nL17@P0: the simulation and possibly modify it so as to accommodate their own perspectives and possibly\nL18@P0: enable their own investigations. Indeed, the critique received from Levin took a unique form—he\nL19@P0: improved the computer-based model such that it better simulates the target constructs. Such co\u0002constructive critique, we argue, is a hallmark of the promise of agent-based modeling. So we\nL20@P0: submit that this proof of existence, if anecdotal validation, may be a harbinger of a new mode of\nL21@P0: research in the learning sciences and beyond—a mode that builds on constructionism (Papert,\nL22@P0: 1991): constructionist collaboration.\nL23@P0: Background\nL24@P0: We have argued (Abrahamson & Wilensky, 2005) that ABM has potential to contribute to the\nL25@P0: advancement of theory in at least three major ways: (a) explicitizing—ABM computational\nL26@P0: environments demand an exacting level of clarity and specificity in expressing a theoretical\nL27@P0: model and provide the tools, structures, and standard practices to achieve this high level; (b)\nL28@P0: emergence—the computational power of ABM enables the researcher to mobilize an otherwise\nL29@P0: static list of conjectured behaviors and witness any group-level patterns that may enfold through\nL30@P0: multiple interactions between the agents who implement these conjectured behaviors; and (c)\nL31@P0: intra/inter-disciplinary collaboration—the lingua franca of ABM enables researchers, who\nL32@P0: otherwise use different frameworks, terminology, and methodologies, to understand and critique\nL33@P0: each others’ theory and even challenge or improve the theory by modifying and/or extending the\nL34@P0: computational procedures that underlie the model. It is the latter attribute of ABM that enabled\nL35@P0: the third author to readily engage in discourse with the first two authors, following their initial\nL36@P0: presentation of the “I’m Game!” Piagetian–Vygotskiian model (Abrahamson & Wilensky, 2005).\nL37@P0: In this paper, we begin by recapping the Abrahamson–Wilensky paper and model (hence, A–W)\nL38@P0: and then present Levin’s proposed improvement on the A–W model as a case study of the A–W\nL39@P0: call for model-based constructive critique. We end with a cautionary remark on the limitations of\nL40@P0: ABM, the importance of recognizing these limitations, and the relevance of this caution for the\nL41@P0: field’s understanding, and prospective incorporation, of ABM as a viable means of propelling\nL42@P0: research into the mechanisms of learning.\nL43@P0: In D. Abrahamson (Organizer), U. Wilensky (Chair), & R. Lesh (Discussant), Learning Complexity: Agent\u0002based modeling supporting education research on student cognition in social contexts. Paper presented at the\nL44@P0-1: annual meeting of the American Educational Research Association, Chicago, IL.Abrahamson, Wilensky, & Levin (2007)\nL45@P1: 2\nL46@P1: The A–W 2005 Paper: ABM as a Methodology in the Service of Theory-of-Learning Research\nL47@P1: Figure 1. Six snapshots from successive experimental runs in the “I’m Game!” model.\nL48@P1: The “I’m Game!” model (see Figure 1, above), built in the NetLogo agent-based modeling\nL49@P1: environment (Wilensky, 1999), implements sketches of “Piagetian” and “Vygotskiian”\nL50@P1: interpretations of human learning into a single model and examines learner–agents’ performance\nL51@P1: under “Piagetian,” “Vygotskiian,” and combined “Piagetian–Vygotskiian” conditions. “Players”\nL52@P1: (circled arrows) stand in a row (Figure 1a). They each roll a marble toward a target line. Some\nL53@P1: players undershoot the line, some overshoot it (Figure 1b). Players collect their marbles, adjust\nL54@P1: the force of their roll—based either on their own performance (“Piagetian”) or on their\nL55@P1: neighbors’ (“Vygotskiian”)—and, on a subsequent trial (Figure 1c), improve on their first trial—\nL56@P1: they have “learned” as individuals. Gradually, the group converges on the target line (see Figure\nL57@P1-2: 1d-1f, for three later attempts).Abrahamson, Wilensky, & Levin (2007)\nL58@P2: 3\nL59@P2: Figure 2. Interface of the “I’m Game!” NetLogo model, with results from multiple runs.\nL60@P2: We have run the model under a wide range of conditions so as to evaluate its capacity to simulate\nL61@P2: reliably core features of the embedded theoretical model. For example, Figure 2 (above) shows\nL62@P2: results from running the simulation within a particular parameter space (see the sliders and\nL63@P2: switches) under the three experimental conditions and a fourth, control condition (“random”).\nL64@P2: Group mean performance (distance from target) was ranked as “Piagetian–Vygotskiian” (nearest,\nL65@P2: so best), “Piagetian,” “Vygotskiian,” and “Random” (furthest, so worst). We thus express one\nL66@P2: possible interpretation of the complementarity of the Piagetian and Vygotskiian perspectives\nL67@P2: (e.g., Cole & Wertsch, 1996).\nL68@P2: Proof of Existence: LCHC Responds to CCL’s Call for ABM-Based Theory-of-Learning\nL69@P2: Collaborative Research\nL70@P2: As part of an effort to study distributed learning, members of the Laboratory of Comparative\nL71@P2: Human Cognition (LCHC) at the University of California, San Diego began using NetLogo as a\nL72@P2: modeling environment for learning and examined the Abrahamson–Wilensky (A–W) model of\nL73@P2: Piagetian and Vygotskiian learning. LCHC draws heavily on cultural historical activity theory\nL74@P2: developed by Vygotsky and others, and so they were especially interested in the expression of\nL75@P2: Vygotskiian learning embedded in the A–W model. The A–W model was introduced at a weekly\nL76@P2: laboratory meeting in several ways. First, the model was shown to the whole group while it ran\nL77@P2: through a series of simulations of learning. The parameters made available through the interface\nL78@P2: were explored. The underlying code was examined, but the amount of code and the relative\nL79@P2: unfamiliarity with NetLogo code by most of the Lab limited the utility of this examination. A\nL80@P2-3: printout of a subset of the code was distributed to the Lab members, with the core codeAbrahamson, Wilensky, & Levin (2007)\nL81@P3: 4\nL82@P3: expressing the theory highlighted. Even though members of LCHC by and large did not know\nL83@P3: how to interpret NetLogo statements, a textual explanation of the core code was given by two\nL84@P3: members of the Lab who were familiar with NetLogo.\nL85@P3: During the discussion, it became clear that the A–W implementation of the concept of “Zone of\nL86@P3: Proximal Development” was “simplex” – that is, learning in the ZPD embodied in the model\nL87@P3: depended on changes by the less skilled member of a pair of learners without any changes by the\nL88@P3: more skilled member. Several members of LCHC pointed out how the dynamic construction of a\nL89@P3: ZPD is “duplex,” that it involves both the learner and the “teacher” (the more skilled person). It\nL90@P3: occurred to Jim Levin that a relatively simple change to the model would implement a way in\nL91@P3: which teachers change their behavior depending on their knowledge of the level at which their\nL92@P3: students are performing. In this way, the members of LCHC were accepting the challenge in the\nL93@P3: Abrahamson & Wilensky (2005) paper describing this model: “We would welcome a critique\nL94@P3: that uses the existing model as a basis for expressing these constructs” (p. 28).\nL95@P3: Figure 3: Interface of the “I'm Game!” A-W model as modified by Jim Levin to improve the\nL96@P3: Vygotskiian learning.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn20view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://repository.upenn.edu/pwpl/vol14/iss1/25/\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://repository.upenn.edu/pwpl/vol14/iss1/25/ is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOxford Textbook of Obstetric Anaesthesia | Oxford Academic (https://academic.oup.com/book/27881)\nciteturn20view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://academic.oup.com/book/27881\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2993\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】 【1†Skip to Main Content】 [Input][Input][Input][Input]\nL1: \nL2:   * Search Menu\nL3:   *   *   * 【2†Im